A||||24||MooCow||Need everyones input (Long)||||4||
Z||000000||MooCow||05-13-2001||11:22 PM||||K, as you know I am having issues with my 87 3.4 Fiero.  My dad sat down with me and we talked about and this is what he told me.  He wants me to get the Fiero fixed, then sell it to pay off my loan.  Then since I have 1 more year of college to go that I should go out and buy a $8-$9 grand Ranger.  I own a 91 Ranger now that is close to 100,000 on it.  I feel it would easily last me another year to get me through college.  Only thing it might need would be a clutch and a new slave if that.  He just gave me $200 to try and get the Fiero fixed. In the shop as I type. I like his idea cause when I graduate college I would not really be in debt at all and have a nice truck, but not a Fiero.  I want to keep my Fiero and get it fixed and trade for an auto one.  I have found people to trade with, but am afraid of being in the same boat I am now. So what would you do if you were 19??  Sell my Fiero get out of debt and then go $8-9 grand in debt?  Or trade my Fiero for an auto one and keep my Ranger another year or so??    <P>                               Thanks Ken<P>||||reg||4
Z||000001||Formula||05-13-2001||11:36 PM||||wont the fiero be as reliable as a 8-9k ranger?<P><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigformulagt.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Haze_Performance||05-13-2001||11:41 PM||||Well...I cant believe I'm saying this but...<BR>19 is pretty young. (Man that made me feel old) OK.. You can always but another Fiero in the future. I would go with the truck until you are out of school & more financially stable....<P><BR>-<I>Haze</I> The old man  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.ws6fiero.com" TARGET=_blank><BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/haze8588logo.gif">      </A>       <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/WS6_Fiero/photo.html" TARGET=_blank><BR>Pennock's Fiero Forum Members Photo Page</A><BR><I>If you want to add your Fiero to the collection, post or send me a photo. (Sent photos ONLY to: purplehazeracing@yahoo.com )</I><P><A HREF="http://www.clubs.yahoo.com/clubs/ws6fiero" TARGET=_blank>WS6 Fiero Club</A>||||reg||11
Z||000003||mrfiero||05-13-2001||11:44 PM||||My thinking is if you can't afford to fix your Fiero how in the world can you afford a $9K truck?  Please don't take this the wrong way, but you would be a fool to get that far into debt before you graduate and get a "real" job.  I say sell the Fiero and keep your older truck.  After you graduate you can sell or trade in the old truck for whatever car you want at that time.<P>When I first went to college back in 1989 I was driving a 10 year old Honda Civic (paid $700 for it).  The following spring (April 1990) I bought a 24K mile '88 GT (paid $9000) because I felt I needed a better looking and more reliable car for school.  Guess what?  I couldn't afford the car and school so I dropped out.......it took me 10 years before I went back and I still don't have my degree yet.  <P>To reitterate.......sell the Fiero and keep the old truck.  DO NOT go into further debt while you are still in school.<P>Just my 2 cents. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||05-14-2001||01:03 AM||||Sell the Fiero?  What in the world for?  How about you sell the truck, put that money into the Fiero so it is more reliable, and forget buying something new.  I drive my Fiero daily, and honestly don't feel it's not reliable.  A Ranger...is that supposed to symbolize reliability???  I'm assuming you think a lot of Rangers since you're considering buying another one, but I have a strong opinion that they are the worst trucks ever produced.  8-9 grand on one...I laugh...no way could you ever convince me of that.  If you're looking for a truck, why not a early 90's S-10.  With a 5 speed, 2.5, they get excellent mileage, and are super-reliable.<P>If I were you, I would sell the ranger and keep the fiero.  The financial reasoning behind this is that the ranger's resale value is plumetting by the second, and in a couple years your fiero will still be worth the same, if not more depending on the effort you put into it to keep it in good shape.  The desire reasoning is..who the hell wants a ranger when you can have a fiero?  Even a brand new ranger?  You can always convince a friend with a truck to haul some stuff for you, but it's hard to convince a friend with a sports car to let you go "haul" ass with his car.<P>There's definitely no point in putting yourself into debt for a new vehicle right now.  That's just one of those "dad is worried about you and doesn't want your car to break down...ever" things; this is a form of parental paranoia when you're in school  I know, because I'm going through the same thing.  I had a 1980 Accord that I got for free, fixed to run for 350 bucks, and put about 13K miles on...they decided that was too much.  So, I got the fiero, a huge UPGRADE for me (upgrade to a reliable car, that is).  Hopefully you don't let Dad talk YOU into putting YOURSELF into that much debt, to make HIMSELF feel comfortable.  If he's not helping to pay for the car, then it's not his decision.<P>Bryce's Final Thoughts: "In this day and age it is hard to decide what the best thing to do with our steadily aging cars is.  We sometimes doubt their ability to be reliable, but comparing costs is the final factor.  If you put one thousand dollars into your car (the cost of a downpayment on a new rig), your car would be just as reliable as the new one.  Furthermore, comparing maintenance costs, fun factor, performance, ego factor, and resale value, the fiero is an obvious winner.  Don't think twice about selling your fiero....think about it as many times as you need to before you come to the realization that you shouldn't do it.  Better yet, don't think about it at all.  Thanks folks, and have a good day."<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||13
Z||000005||87GTZ34||05-14-2001||01:54 AM||||Uhmm MrFiero, that is exactly what dad was trying to do.  Now you've uncovered his plot and spoiled the plan...  Moo, I'm even older than Haze (ouch!) and I'd have to agree w/ him.  If you purchase another Fiero chances are it won't be in mint shape and they always need somthin'.  Most of the Fieros that I've seen have been abused and neglected (yes that's right, not everyone appreciates them like we do), so your psychic would probably tell you that repairs are in your near future when she finds out you own a Fiero.  I bought mine for a cheap project car and guess what?  The car was but parts aren't and I do the labor myself.<P>GL and choose wisely||||reg||1
Z||000006||thomas_l||05-14-2001||06:59 AM||||Pick one of the cars and go with it. If you like the Fiero better than the truck keep it and dump the pickup. Sounds like a new car/truck was not an option, so you will be spending maintenance $$$ no matter what. I finished up my college (GSU '99) in a 77 280Z followed by a 77 MB 450SEL. They both took a bit of time & money to keep running. The secret is to reduce your driving and they won't tear up as quick. Oh yeah - try to avoid too many ugrade$  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000007||ChadMan||05-14-2001||08:50 AM||||I'm thinking your payment on an $8500 Ranger would be around $230/mo or so for 4 years.  That's a lot of jack for a used vehicle that might have its own problems...<P>I'd do one of the following:<P>1)  Keep your existing cars<BR>2)  Sell your existing cars and lease something brand-new and economical.  Seems like they've always got some great lease deal on Cavaliers, Corollas or Saturns.  Yeah, I know, they're not especially fun or cool, but you know they won't break down (and if they do, they're under warranty).  You can lease a new Saturn SL1 right now for $199/mo with $0 down.  That sounds like a better deal than a 2nd-hand Ranger to me.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||MooCow||05-14-2001||09:13 AM||||I do know for sure I would rather have the Fiero over a Ranger any day.  I also know that I have lots complete faith in my current Fiero.  I'm thinking that I am going to keep my Ranger and get the Fiero fixed and try to either trade it or sell it and get another one.  Other thing is that I have been in debt ever since I was 16 with my first car, so going in debt is nothing new to me.  I just dont want to buy a newer Ranger cause then that means no Fiero for a long time.  Thanks for the help thus far.  I'll let my dad know how I feel and will see what he says.<BR>p.s. How do you see Fiero's??  A hobby, or daily driver??<P>                              Thanks Ken||||reg||14
