Z||000010||Fiero5||05-14-2001||02:14 PM||||I would stay with the Fiero too. Cheaper to maintain and great on gas.<P>Steve<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fiero3_3.jpg">         <BR>http://www.kelleyfamilyfieros.homestead.com/index.html||||reg||6
Z||000011||sqoach||05-14-2001||02:35 PM||||Hmm...why would you want to go in more debt to buy a newer pick-up? And why a Ranger?? I agree with Nascho on the S-10s. Great little pick-ups. My brother had one for nearly two years and I can't remember it ever having a problem. And I drove it quite a bit. They're nice and get good gas mileage. I would actually like to get one.<BR>Anyway, if you're looking for reliability, you should go for the leasing idea. If something happens, the warranty is there to make it all better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>But since I like Fieros, I'd fine a nice low-mileage Fiero in good shape. I just don't see the point in throwing 8 or 9 grand into something if that's not really want you want.<P>Ok there's my opinion. Not sure if it all made sense, but oh well.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000012||Ken Wittlief||05-14-2001||03:06 PM||||What exactly are you taking in college that you need to drive a truck to school!?<P>I dont know how things are in your part of the world, but here in upstate NY you can get yourself a decent reliable car for about $3000 that will last you a few years. <P>If you buy anyting for less than that you can expect to put in the difference in repairs and maintaince yourself, like the 85SE I just bought for $1500. I figure I have will have it running well and in solid shape by the time I have spent another $1500 on it.<P>Cars are complex mechanical systems, and you cant find one just lying around for $300 (unless its stolen).<P>I've done it both ways: I've purchased brand new cars and put 160k miles or more on them, and I have bought cars for $50 and restored them and drove them for years.<P>To be honest with you, I like buying the used cars and fixing them up better. Heres why. When they mold the sheet metal and parts in the factory for a new car it starts slowly rusting immediately. Everytime you run your car it wears a little. So when you buy a new car it has nowhere to go but downhill, as you own it and drive it it will get worse and worse, till its time for major repairs. <P>and for most of us a new car mean a loan. If you get a $12,000 car on a 4 year loan you normally end up paying around $15,000 total by the time the car is payed off, then you have a 4 year old car you payed $15,000 for.<P>But if you buy an older used car (like a fiero) you can usually pay cash for it, so you are not paying interest to anyone. And it will need work, so as you own it you are fixing it up, and it is getting better and better, not worse and worse. <P>If you start off with a $2000 car and put another $13,000 into it over 4 years, what will you have 4 years from now? One hot little, solid, better than new machine.<P> besides that you really learn a lot, and understand things on a deeper level when you restore and maintain your own vehicle.<P>  One exception to this is you gotta have the time to work on the machine. If you are some kinda math wizard or computer genius, or are going to medical school, then you would be better off focusing on your career and studies, and paying someone else (detroit) to be the mechanic for you. I dont want this to sound trite. Some people are gifted, and it would be a waste for them to spend their time learning to fix up a car if they could be doing more important things with their time.<P>  But for most of us, the knowledge and skills and understanding you gain by restoring or maintaining at least one car in your 'youth', will stay with you through the rest of your life. there is nothing sadder than talking to a 40 year old guy who has no idea what an overhead cam is, or how fuel injection works, or who cant do a simple maintaince job like change an oil filter or disk brake pads.||||reg||1
Z||000013||sqoach||05-14-2001||03:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ken Wittlief:<BR><B>Cars are complex mechanical systems, and you cant find one just lying around for $300 (unless its stolen).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>On the contrary (however you spell it)<P>My dad bought a Mercury Topaz for $150 last summer. I think all it needed was a fuel filter. Other than the crappy paint and crappy interior, it's not too bad. It's more reliable than my car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Hasn't had any real problems, just regular maintenance.<P>Anyway, just thought I'd share.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||p4n1c||05-14-2001||04:04 PM||||Don't feel bad, because I'm almost 21 and just got my car.  And it's a hand me down from my dad.  And I don't even have it here.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It's still at my parents place.  I won't have it until second week of June.<P>I'm waiting until I have a house of my own and somewhere to work on a Fiero until I get one and by then I'll hopefully be able to afford one that I want.<BR><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International||||reg||1
Z||000015||MooCow||05-14-2001||05:12 PM||||Well thus far my Ranger has been by far teh most reliable car I have owned.  In the 2 years I have owned it I have only put $200 in repairs on it.  I also get good gas and it is a V6 so I have the power to tow things if need be.  I do know that I can't just buy another Fiero.  I have to have a winter car of some sort.  I am now leaning twords getting my Fiero fixed and either selling it for an auto or just keeping it.  Also keeping the Ranger.  So I have a summer car and a winter truck.  Then when I graduate Baker College I can buy a newer Ranger then.<BR>Thanks for all the replies thus far.<P>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||14
Z||000016||Cheever2||05-14-2001||05:32 PM||||Haven't read all the other replies yet, but I'm leaning toward advising you to sell the Fiero, for 3 reasons: *-Pay off that loan; **-Keep Dad happy; and ***-There will be other affordable Fieros in a couple of years if you want to get another one.  Maybe make your buyer promise to give you the chance to buy it back if he/she ever wants to sell it.  That's what the owner of my Coupe did to me, and I'm sticking to my promise.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Nashco||05-15-2001||02:54 AM||||Hmm...I guess only some people have open ears.  I thought I made my case quite well, but apparently not.  What part should I go over again?  The financial reasons, the desire reasons, or the don't do it part?  Hmm...I guess we all have our opinions.<P>Since you seem stuck on getting rid of your current Fiero no matter what the Oijua board tells you and Dad, perhaps you should at least reconsider the newer Ranger.  Seriously, an early 90's S-10 is going to have the same reliability as a late 90's Ranger.  My dad had an 85 2.5 4 speed that we got for free (<B>Yes Ken, that's less than 300 bucks, and not even stolen!</B>), put a head gasket and muffler on it, and drove it for 20k miles.  He then put a water pump on it, and after about another 10k miles decided to sell it because he got another truck in a trade.  He sold the S-10 for 1200 bucks.  Now, if this doesn't make sense, I don't know what the hell does.  Can you say that you made 700 dollars profit on your last vehicle after 30k miles?  I didn't think so.  There are plenty of cheap cars out there, don't let Dad fool you into thinking they aren't good cars because they're cheap.  That's what the fools who're selling them for a hundred bucks thought too; why don't you ask them how they're liking payments that are the same cost per month as my last car was per year.<P>Don't get sucked any further down the money pit Moo...I played that road from 15 until 19, and that's it for me.  I'm debt free now, and I don't plan on having any more until I buy a house.  It just doesn't make sense to pay that much for a warranty....8 months of a lease payment is more money than you could possibly need to get yourself into a better situation.  Don't cut to the chase, think about this one.<P>Hopefully there are some more people to back me, and maybe you will change the direction you're headed in soon.  Keep us updated!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000018||Uaana||05-15-2001||03:23 AM||||OK I'm gonna back Nacho on this one. <BR>Since I'm in the upper half of this board age wise you have to listen to me. <BR>And because I too have been down the perilous world of credit.. <BR>A. Sell the Fiero, pay off loan.<BR>B. Keep older ranger no matter how big a POS <BR>   it is. <BR>C. Forget about newer ranger even used there <BR>   is something everyone else forgot if you <BR>   get a loan for a veihicle you have to pay<BR>   full coverage INS on it. Your young so <BR>   increase your car payment by 25% to cover <BR>   the INS increase.<BR>D. Graduate college.<BR>E. Meet pretty girl, get married buy house.<BR>F. Fully stock garage with every tool you <BR>   Will ever need.<BR>G. Buy poor stranded, abused Fiero. RESTORE!<P>You may modify E-G as fits. But please don't bury yourself in more debt before finishing college.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Uaana||05-15-2001||03:25 AM||||BTW I'll buy your 3.4 for 2,500. <BR>Sorry it's all I have. And since you might be in the market, had to try.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||11:32 AM||||Sure there are alway exceptions, some people get lucky and end up with a great vehicle for a song. And some people win the lottery too, but you cant plan on those things.<P>  In the long run a car costs you about 30 cents a mile to drive. For the average person thats $1500 / year. So if you are looking for a car that is gonna last you 2 years you are looking at $3000 worth the car, more or less.<P>  Sure there are exceptions, someone who doesnt know what they have and lets it go for nothing, repo and police auctions, buying a great car with a blown engine for $300 and buying a wrecked car with a great engine for $300...<P>   But going this route takes time, time to shop around, time to check out the cars, time to make the repairs, if you keep your eyes open you can get a good deal every once in a while. Figure $20 an hour for the time you are investing in all this and it adds up too.<P>  Those are the exceptions. you guys may think Im silly about this, but if I went to see a car that someone was selling, and it was worth a few thousand dollars, and they only wanted a few hundred for it, I would feel obliged to point out to them the actual value of the car. If I wanted to steal someones car, I would just steal it.<P><BR>  You may think they are stupid for not knowing the value of what they have. Or maybe the car belonged to a lost loved one, or maybe that person is going through a personal crisis. <P>  If for whatever reason someone is willing to give you a $3000 car for $300 and you take advantage of the situation then <P>  then I dont know what? What do you call someone who takes advantage of other peoples mistakes, shortcomings, or oversights?||||reg||1
Z||000021||MooCow||05-15-2001||11:33 AM||||Well my Ranger to me is pretty good and definetly not a pos.  I have faith that the truck will last another year with minor problems.  I can't see myself not having a Fiero for another whole year or more.  I will go crazy.  I am going crazy right now and my Fiero is just down the road.  I am tempted to keep my Fiero, but do not trust it anymore after all that has happened to it.  If anything I would rather trade it so I know I still have a Fiero and have my winter/backup truck.  I just can't decide if I want to keep my Fiero or trade it.  Man I wish I could travel into time and see what would happen in the decisions I amke now to see which one is the better choice.<BR>                             Thanks Everyone<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig.jpg"> <BR>87 GT, 3.4 engine with a 4:10 tranny, and Viper Yellow!||||reg||10
Z||000022||pontiacs4me||05-15-2001||12:48 PM||||then sell the truck, pay off as much debt as you can with that money, fix the fiero, and drive it.  And I can attest that fieros are reliable, I just drove mine to California and back in 1 week.  No problems at all.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||01:46 PM||||ok, one view point from your dads perspective.<P>If you are doing all the work on your car and are taking good care of it, and it only ends up inthe shop once in a while, thats one thing<P>but if the car is breaking down frequently, and being towed, and you (or dad) is paying someone else to do all the repairs, then that is a different story.<P>Lots of people own cars that are high-maintaince. Thats their choice, like MGs for example. But if someone else is doing all the fixup work, or paying for all the fixup work, then you really cant complain if they say, "enough already!"<P> or if you are frequently bugging your dad for rides, or borrowing his wheels cause yours are offline - same situation.<P>  If you want to keep the fiero, to be fair to your dad, you need to take responsibility for keeping it running (part time job after school?) and you need to find your own transportation when its not running (bus, bike, car pooling...)||||reg||1
Z||000024||87GTZ34||05-15-2001||10:31 PM||||Moo, your father is simply trying to take vehicles out of the equation so you can focus on school.  This is not unwise.  Cars & trucks will be around for your lifetime but you most likely will get your associate's or bachelor's degree only once. 'be all you can be' on that point.  Also, if you have confidence in a vehicle, I would not make a decision on someone elses experiences (sample size 1 or 2).  For instance I had a brand new '96 blazer and loved it.  It was a lemon and chevrolet motor division tried to make me eat it.  Long story short, they swallowed hard but they ate it (all of it). I do not think all chevy's or blazer's are a POS based on sample size 1. Only the witch manager of CMD's cust service group is a POS.   Anyway if you are confident with your current truck keep it. Compromise w/ dad - if the Fiero dies again, you will store it (or sell it if you decide to) until you're out of school and have time and money to put into it.||||reg||1
